# Rust build artifacts
/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# `Cargo.lock` **is committed**, deliberately.
#
# The usual advice — ignore it for libraries — applies to crates that are only
# ever consumed as dependencies, where the resolver is free to pick newer
# compatible versions. This workspace produces a binary that is released, and for
# that the lockfile is the difference between "the release was built from these
# versions" and "the release was built from whatever was current that day".
#
# It also makes `cargo build --locked` meaningful, which is what the release
# workflow uses. Without a committed lockfile that flag has nothing to check
# against and the build fails before it starts.

# Local toolchain cache used when the global CARGO_HOME is not writable
/.cargo/

# pnpm's content-addressable store.
#
# It is 376 MB and it is a *cache*: every file in it is reproducible from the
# lockfile, and none of it belongs in a repository. It appears at the workspace
# root when pnpm resolves a store relative to the workspace rather than to the
# user's home — which is what happened here, and how 16,499 files reached a
# commit before anyone noticed.
#
# Listed at every level a store can appear, because which one pnpm picks depends
# on how it was invoked.
.pnpm-store/
**/.pnpm-store/
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
